# Repository-owned line endings (issue #64).
#
# Without this file, git falls back to each developer's `core.autocrlf`. The
# Git for Windows installer offers `core.autocrlf=true` as its default, which
# rewrites every LF to CRLF at checkout time — so a pristine Windows clone
# fails `gofmt -l` (scripts/verify.sh) before anyone has edited a line, and
# bash refuses the CRLF shell scripts. Declaring the rules here makes the
# checkout a property of the repository instead of a property of the machine.
#
# Guarded by tests/repohygiene/line_endings_test.go, which re-materialises the
# tracked files through git with core.autocrlf=true forced on and asserts the
# result is LF and gofmt-clean. That test runs on both the Linux and the
# Windows CI jobs (`go test ./...`), and `scripts/verify.sh` additionally
# checks the developer's own working tree.

# Default: let git detect text vs binary, normalise text to LF in the index,
# and check text out as LF on every platform. The index is already all-LF, so
# this normalises nothing that exists today — it only pins the future.
* text=auto eol=lf

# --- Explicit rules for the file types the quality gates depend on ----------
# Redundant with the catch-all above, on purpose: these are the ones whose
# breakage is a build failure, so they should survive a careless edit of the
# catch-all line.
*.go        text eol=lf
*.sh        text eol=lf
*.mod       text eol=lf
*.sum       text eol=lf
Makefile    text eol=lf
Dockerfile  text eol=lf
*.yml       text eol=lf
*.yaml      text eol=lf

# PowerShell: LF. Deliberate. Windows PowerShell 5.1 and PowerShell 7 both
# execute LF-only scripts, the repository's only .ps1
# (labs/repeat-scan-wrappers/windows/scan-loop.ps1) is LF today and is linted
# in a Linux container, and a single LF rule keeps the regression check simple.
# Alternative, if this project ever ships Authenticode-signed scripts: switch
# to `*.ps1 text eol=crlf`, because a signature block appended by
# Set-AuthenticodeSignature assumes CRLF.
*.ps1       text eol=lf

# Batch files: CRLF. Deliberate, and the one exception to LF-everywhere.
# cmd.exe parses a .bat/.cmd file line by line as it executes it, and its
# handling of labels (goto targets) and multi-line constructs depends on the
# CR; LF-only batch files fail in ways that are hard to diagnose. These must
# reach a Windows disk as CRLF regardless of which platform committed them.
# The repository has no batch file today; the rule is here so the first one
# added is correct by default.
*.bat       text eol=crlf
*.cmd       text eol=crlf

# --- Generated / binary artifacts ------------------------------------------
# `binary` is git's built-in macro for `-text -diff`: never EOL-converted (a
# CRLF rewrite would corrupt them) and never diffed as text. `text=auto` above
# would already auto-detect these, but detection is a heuristic and a corrupted
# committed binary is silent — so the committed build outputs are explicit.
dist/**     binary
*.exe       binary
*.pptx      binary
*.png       binary
*.jpg       binary
*.jpeg      binary
*.gif       binary
*.ico       binary
*.pdf       binary
*.zip       binary
*.gz        binary
